00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:06.150 align:start position:0%
until recently the small city of sehana  in the southeastern state of sao paulo  was no different to the rest of brazil  with covid19 infections spreading at  alarming rates  hospitals on the brink of collapse and  not enough vaccines  for its population of 45 000  that changed in february when sehana was  chosen  for a pioneer study to discover what  would happen if  every adult was vaccinated  four months later ninety-seven percent  of all adults have received two doses of  the coronavac vaccine  produced by china and sao paulo's  institute and sahana has become an oasis  of health  in a country with the world's second  largest coven 19 death toll  the results of the experiment were  announced on monday  this unprecedented study shows that the  number of deaths dropped 95  the number of hospitalizations dropped  86  and the number of people who were  infected with the virus and felt  symptoms  dropped by 80 percent this is good news  for brazil and the world  the study shows that inoculating 75  of the adult population is enough to  control the coronavirus  even the more aggressive p1 variant  which first appeared in the brazilian  city of manaus  children up to 18 years old were not  vaccinated  but the study found that they had been  indirectly protected  by the wide scale inoculation program  and could return to schools safely  santa's success story is certainly good  news but not a guarantee that the rest  of brazil will  follow in its footsteps president jain  bolsonaro has downplayed the virus from  the start  and criticized the chinese vaccine  promoted by  sao paulo's governor who is a strong  contender  in the 2022 presidential elections  meanwhile another city of sao paulo  state is vaccinating  all residents between 18 and 60 years  old  but the study in botocatu is being  carried out with the astrazeneca vaccine  produced by oxford and brazil's fucrus  institute  so it's absolutely vital for all of us  across the world that we have as much  data as possible and the exp  and and the research that is being done  in botocateu over the next months  is going to be really vital for that  scientific sharing  an increase of knowledge in the world  about how the astrazeneca vaccine  operates  for many brazilians receiving a vaccine  is still a long way off  so far only 10 percent of the population  has been fully inoculated monika yanakiv  al jazeera rio de janeiro
 

